{% extends 'layouts/application.njk' %}

{% block head %}
<link rel="canonical" href="https://jakeflem.com/resume/">
<meta name="robots" content="noindex">
{% endblock %}

{% block content %}

<main class="resume">
  <section class="section">
    <div class="container text-center">
      <div class="stw p-relative">
        <h1>
          <span class="resize d-block" style="padding: 0 20%;">Jake</span>
          <span class="resize d-block">Fleming</span>
        </h1>
        <h2>Product Designer</h2>
      </div>
      <p class="lead">
        I design tools for technical people, and I build a lot of them myself.
      </p>
      <p class="resume-meta">San Francisco Bay Area &middot; <a href="mailto:jcbflmng@gmail.com">jcbflmng@gmail.com</a> &middot; <a href="https://www.linkedin.com/in/jakeflem/">LinkedIn</a> &middot; <a href="https://github.com/jakefleming">GitHub</a></p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="section">
    <div class="container">
      <h4 class="text-center">The short</h4>
      <div class="stw p-relative">
        <h3 class="text-center resize">Version</h3>
      </div>
      <ul>
        <li>Fifteen years of product design. Most of it on software where the user is an engineer, a researcher, or an analyst and the screen is full of data.</li>
        <li>Five and a half years running design at Labelbox, a data labeling and model evaluation platform.</li>
        <li>I can write production code (HTML, CSS, TypeScript, Swift), but these days I mostly don't have to. I build with frontier models: I decide what gets made and how it should work, read what comes back, and ship it. Hellbox and blinkdiff were built that way.</li>
        <li>A lot of my work has the same shape: a system flags something, and a person has to decide whether it's real and what to do about it. I've designed that screen at Metromile, at Labelbox, and in blinkdiff.</li>
        <li>I have managed a design team and I have been the only designer in the building. Either way I stay close to the work.</li>
        <li>When I want a tool that doesn't exist, I build it. A few of those have paying customers now.</li>
      </ul>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="section">
    <div class="container">
      <h4 class="text-center">Where I've</h4>
      <div class="stw p-relative">
        <h3 class="text-center resize">Worked</h3>
      </div>

      <div class="job">
        <div class="job-head"><span class="job-co"><a href="https://attilio.com">Attilio</a></span><span class="job-role">Founding Product Designer</span><span class="job-when">Dec 2025 &ndash; now</span></div>
        <p>First designer at a health and longevity startup. I own the product design and a good chunk of the front end. Built the design system that keeps a tiny team consistent across a client app, an advisor workspace, and an admin tool. Set up feature flags and experiments (GrowthBook) early so we measure launches instead of arguing about them.</p>
        <p>I wanted screenshot diffs on every pull request for the mobile app and nothing did it well for React Native, so I built <a href="https://blinkdiff.com">blinkdiff</a>. More on that below.</p>
      </div>

      <div class="job">
        <div class="job-head"><span class="job-co">Cortex</span><span class="job-role">Principal Product Designer</span><span class="job-when">May 2025 &ndash; Dec 2025</span></div>
        <p>Cortex is an engineering intelligence platform: a catalog of every service a company runs, scorecards for how healthy they are, and the dashboards engineering leaders look at to decide what to fix. The users are engineers and the data is about their own systems. I was the principal designer.</p>
      </div>

      <div class="job">
        <div class="job-head"><span class="job-co">Labelbox</span><span class="job-role">VP of Design &larr; Head of Design &larr; Principal &larr; Lead</span><span class="job-when">Feb 2020 &ndash; Jun 2025</span></div>
        <p>Joined as the lead designer, ended up VP, stayed hands-on the whole time. Labelbox is a data labeling and model evaluation platform, so everything I designed was for people staring at a lot of data: annotation and review queues at scale, querying and curating datasets, comparing model output to human output, quality and throughput dashboards, and the permissions and audit trail that make a label something you'd actually train on. I built the design system and hired and ran the team while the whole AI field took off around us.</p>
      </div>

      <div class="job">
        <div class="job-head"><span class="job-co">Metromile</span><span class="job-role">Senior Product Designer</span><span class="job-when">Aug 2019 &ndash; Feb 2020</span></div>
        <p>Designed the fraud-detection tools for Metromile Enterprise. An ML model flags a claim; an investigator has to decide if it's real. My job was the screen in between: the evidence, how confident the model was, and what to do next.</p>
      </div>

      <div class="job">
        <div class="job-head"><span class="job-co">Tradecraft</span><span class="job-role">Product Design Track Lead</span><span class="job-when">Jul 2015 &ndash; Aug 2019</span></div>
        <p>Ran the product design program for four years. Wrote the curriculum, taught it, placed a lot of people at startups. Teaching something is the fastest way to find out if you understand it.</p>
      </div>

      <div class="job">
        <div class="job-head"><span class="job-co">Before that</span><span class="job-role">Appiphony, Piasa Games, Big Lobby</span><span class="job-when">2010 &ndash; 2015</span></div>
        <p>Enterprise software on Salesforce. An indie game studio I co-founded (we made Grilly The Cheese). And a first startup job where I did the UI, the branding, the illustration, the animation, and slept on an air mattress at the office.</p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="section">
    <div class="container">
      <h4 class="text-center">Things I</h4>
      <div class="stw p-relative">
        <h3 class="text-center resize">Built</h3>
      </div>
      <ul>
        <li><a href="https://blinkdiff.com">blinkdiff</a>: visual diffing for iOS and macOS apps on every pull request. A CLI, a GitHub Action, and a bot you tell "approve" when the change was on purpose. It drives your real app, screenshots it, and diffs against baselines in your repo. Most of the work was making it deterministic, because a flaky check is worse than no check.</li>
        <li><a href="https://hellbox.com">Hellbox</a>: a Mac app for type designers. It watches your font file and re-renders the proofs every time you save, and double-clicking a glyph in the proof opens it in your editor. Glyphs put it on their homepage. I also built myself a dashboard for it that forecasts which trials will convert and plays a sound when someone buys.</li>
        <li>Both of those have MCP servers, so an agent can run them. I build with agents all day, so I have opinions about how they and engineers should split the work.</li>
        <li>Also: <a href="https://designercize.com">Designercize</a> (design interview prompts), <a href="https://www.futurefonts.xyz/jake-fleming/boxout">Boxout</a> and <a href="https://www.futurefonts.xyz/jake-fleming/goiters">Goiters</a> (typefaces), and <a href="https://store.steampowered.com/app/858530/Cat_Lady/">Cat Lady</a> (a dungeon crawler on Steam).</li>
      </ul>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="section">
    <div class="container">
      <h4 class="text-center">Where I</h4>
      <div class="stw p-relative">
        <h3 class="text-center resize">Studied</h3>
      </div>
      <ul>
        <li>Southern Illinois University, Carbondale: B.A., Graphic Design</li>
        <li>Robert Morris University, Illinois: B.B.A., Business Administration and Management</li>
      </ul>
      <p class="text-center" style="margin-top:2rem;">
        <a class="btn btn-primary btn-lg btn-wide-mobile" href="mailto:jcbflmng@gmail.com">Get In Touch</a>
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>
</main>

{% endblock %}
